Thibaut Mattio announced
=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=
=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=
=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80

  Hi everyone!

  I'm happy to announce the release of [Windtrap], a testing framework
  for OCaml that brings the different testing methods of the ecosystem
  into one library:

  =E2=80=A2 Unit tests, with an Alcotest-like API.
  =E2=80=A2 Expect and snapshot tests, with a companion PPX compatible with
    `ppx_expect' and `ppx_inline_test', but usable without it.
  =E2=80=A2 Property tests as seen in [QCheck], with a library of textbook =
laws
    (round trips, orders, associativity, and more).
  =E2=80=A2 Stateful and differential tests, as seen in [qcheck-stm] and
    [Monolith], on one domain or several.
  =E2=80=A2 Test coverage (as with [Bisect_ppx]) and mutation testing (as w=
ith
    [mutaml]).

  Every kind of test composes with the others into one suite and one
  output, and the library has *no dependencies* beyond OCaml (the
  optional PPX uses ppxlib).

  Here's a small suite that tests the standard library with a unit test,
  a property stated as a textbook law, and a stateful test of `Stack'
  against a list:

  =E2=94=8C=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80
  =E2=94=82 open Windtrap
  =E2=94=82=20
  =E2=94=82 (* A reference for Stack: a list, its top first. *)
  =E2=94=82 module Model =3D struct
  =E2=94=82   type t =3D int list ref
  =E2=94=82=20
  =E2=94=82   let create () =3D ref []
  =E2=94=82   let push x m =3D m :=3D x :: !m
  =E2=94=82   let length m =3D List.length !m
  =E2=94=82=20
  =E2=94=82   let pop m =3D
  =E2=94=82     match !m with
  =E2=94=82     | [] -> raise Stack.Empty
  =E2=94=82     | x :: rest ->
  =E2=94=82         m :=3D rest;
  =E2=94=82         x
  =E2=94=82 end
  =E2=94=82=20
  =E2=94=82 let stack =3D abstract "s" ~pp:(fun ppf m -> Testable.pp (list =
int) ppf !m)
  =E2=94=82=20
  =E2=94=82 let lists =3D
  =E2=94=82   group "list"
  =E2=94=82     [
  =E2=94=82       test "rev reverses a list" (fun () ->
  =E2=94=82           equal (list int) [ 3; 2; 1 ] (List.rev [ 1; 2; 3 ]));
  =E2=94=82       prop "rev is an involution" (Gen.list Gen.int)
  =E2=94=82         (Law.involutive (list int) List.rev);
  =E2=94=82     ]
  =E2=94=82=20
  =E2=94=82 let stacks =3D
  =E2=94=82   group "stack"
  =E2=94=82     [
  =E2=94=82       stateful "behaves like a list"
  =E2=94=82         [
  =E2=94=82           command "create" (Gen.unit @-> makes stack) Model.cre=
ate Stack.create;
  =E2=94=82           command "push"
  =E2=94=82             (Gen.int @-> stack ^-> returns unit)
  =E2=94=82             Model.push Stack.push;
  =E2=94=82           command "pop" (stack ^-> returns int) Model.pop Stack=
.pop;
  =E2=94=82           command "length" (stack ^-> returns int) Model.length=
 Stack.length;
  =E2=94=82         ];
  =E2=94=82     ]
  =E2=94=82=20
  =E2=94=82 let () =3D exit (run "stdlib" [ lists; stacks ])
  =E2=94=94=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80

  We built Windtrap at [Invariant] as part of our work on making code
  written by agents more trustworthy. Agents replicate what they find in
  a repository, so a project full of strong tests gets more of
  them. Windtrap comes with an [agent skill] that guides agents towards
  the right kind of test, but we care just as much about humans, and put
  a lot of effort into making the API and output ergonomic and
  delightful.

  You can read more about it in the [announcement post]. It walks
  through an example suite, what a failure looks like, coverage and
  mutation testing, and an experiment where we used Windtrap to test the
  OCaml standard library.

  To install it (when [the PR] is merged on opam-repository):

  =E2=94=8C=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80
  =E2=94=82 opam install windtrap ppx_windtrap=20
  =E2=94=94=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80=E2=94=80

  The [manual] (written by agents with my guidance, I hope you'll find
  the writing voice bearable, but if enough people complain I'll look
  into rewriting it by hand) has one page per kind of test. Feedback is
  very welcome, here or on the [issue tracker].

  Happy hacking!

  =E2=80=94


